<h2 id="medium" style="color:#1565c0;font-size:1.22em;font-weight:650;border-left:4px solid #42a5f5;padding-left:10px;margin-top:1.1em">1. Pick the right medium</h2>

<table>
<thead>
<tr style="background:#1565c0;color:white"><th>Medium</th><th>Use for</th><th>Avoid for</th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td style="background:#e3f2fd"><strong>ASCII wireframe</strong></td><td style="background:#e8f5e9">Screen layout · app chrome · nav rows · tabs · toolbars · footers · before/after nav</td><td>API schemas · CI pipelines · deep state machines</td></tr>
<tr><td style="background:#e3f2fd"><strong>Mermaid</strong></td><td style="background:#e8f5e9">User flows · IA tree · decision trees · cross-page journeys · logical grouping · dependency order</td><td>Pixel layout (use ASCII instead)</td></tr>
<tr><td style="background:#e3f2fd"><strong>Markdown table</strong></td><td style="background:#fff3e0">Route map · label → URL · role matrix · content source mapping</td><td>Spatial layout</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

**Rule of thumb:** If the reader needs to **see where things sit on the page**, use ASCII. If they need to **see how choices or pages connect**, use Mermaid. Always pair ASCII with a **route/label table** when URLs matter.

**Do not** use screenshots alone as the spec — add ASCII or structured tables.

